CREATED:20260622T133651Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260622T133651Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315313559@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260919T042427Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T140000
SUMMARY:Landmarks 5: Mechanics' Institute to Reference Library via Mackenz
 ie House!
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Yearly Pass
  is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, emai
 l**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for sign
 ing up!**\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount whe
 n booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following em
 ail at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nToronto is a city that hid
 es its history in plain sight. Beneath the glass towers are layers of stor
 ies — grand hotels that vanished overnight\, political scandals whispere
 d through stone corridors\, theatres that once glittered under gaslight\, 
 and laneways where the city first learned how to become a metropolis.\n\nT
 his new Toronto History Walks tour invites the walker to slow down\, and s
 ee the city differently.\n\nOver the course of 30 carefully chosen stops\,
  this walking tour traces the landmarks that helped shape the history of T
 oronto. Beginning at the site of the legendary Holland House — once one 
 of the city’s finest mansions\, and a gathering place for politicians\, 
 and businessmen\, the route winds through the old heart of downtown\, unco
 vering stories both famous and forgotten.\n\nSome locations are grand and 
 imposing. Others are easy to walk past without a second glance. Every stop
  reveals a piece of Toronto’s personality. At Osgoode Hall\, visitors wi
 ll stand before one of the city’s most elegant historic buildings. Nearb
 y\, the old banks and newspaper buildings of the financial district recall
  an era when Toronto proudly called itself “Toronto the Good\,” even a
 s corruption and backroom dealings simmered beneath the surface.\n\nThe to
 ur also explores the vanished city — the Toronto that survives only in p
 hotographs\, and memory. Walkers will discover where magnificent Victorian
  structures once stood before demolition crews erased entire blocks in the
  name of progress. Former theatres\, hotels\, markets\, and department sto
 res emerge again through storytelling\, helping modern explorers imagine t
 he bustle\, smoke\, and noise of earlier generations.\n\nStories of fire\,
  disaster\, celebration\, and reinvention. Participants will hear how the 
 Great Fire of 1904 transformed downtown streets\, how immigrant communitie
 s reshaped neighbourhoods block by block\, and how Toronto repeatedly rein
 vented itself through booms\, depressions\, wars\, and waves of newcomers.
 \n\nBut this tour is not simply about dates and buildings. It is about peo
 ple. The politicians who argued over Confederation. The hotel clerks who w
 itnessed scandals. The newspaper editors who shaped public opinion. The sh
 opkeepers\, labourers\, entertainers\, and ordinary Torontonians whose liv
 es unfolded along these same streets.\n\nToronto has always had hidden cor
 ners and strange tales: tunnels beneath streets\, forgotten cemeteries ben
 eath office towers\, unsolved crimes\, and buildings said to carry echoes 
 of the past. Even longtime residents may find themselves surprised by what
  has been quietly standing before them all along. Come along and experienc
 e a very different city. A deep dive into our history.\n\nThis tour explor
 es the history of the Mechanics' Institute and the area from Church and Ki
 ng Street and publishing row to the 1977 Reference Library. Our mid way st
 op is at Mackenzie House and the history around one of the leading events 
 in Toronto and Canada\; the Rebellion of 1837. We continue north to explor
 e the area between Dundas Street East and Bloor Street East and the landma
 rks\, that gave rise to the district east of Yonge Street. Hope to see you
  there.\n\nThis walking tour begins at the **dog fountain** at Berczy Park
  at the northeast **corner** of Scott Street and Wellington. We end at the
  Toronto Reference Library on Yonge just steps north of Bloor.\n\nThis wal
 king tour is for all walking levels. Please dress for the season\, and hop
 e to see you there.\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\
 n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking 
 your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\
 n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315313
 559/
